  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · Start here</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">First: OF OF isn't a breakdown — it's demo mode.</h2>

    <p><strong>The single most-panicked "code" on a Samsung fridge is OF OF (also shown as O FF or scrolling bars), and it isn't a fault at all. It's Cooling Off / Demo mode — the showroom setting that lights the panel but doesn't cool. People switch it on by accident and think the fridge failed. Exit it by holding the right button combination for about 8 seconds until you hear a chime (the buttons vary by model). The real fault codes are usually a number plus E or C: 22E/21E (fans), 5E (defrost sensor), the 39E/26E ice-maker family, 88 88 (display glitch), and PC ER (communication). Many clear with a defrost or a power cycle; sensors and compressor codes need a tech. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p>Before you assume the worst, check for demo mode — it's the fastest "repair" there is, and it's free. If the panel is lit but nothing's cooling and you see OF OF, that's almost certainly all it is.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 FAN + DEFROST -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · Fan and defrost codes</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The cooling faults.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>22E / 22C — refrigerator fan</h3>
      <p>The fridge evaporator fan isn't reporting rotation, usually because frost has locked the blade or the motor failed. Power off and manual-defrost for about 24 hours, check the vents, and restart. Returns = tech.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>21E — freezer fan</h3>
      <p>The same fault on the freezer evaporator fan. Frost buildup or a failed motor. Same homeowner defrost attempt; a persistent 21E is a tech job.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>5E — defrost sensor</h3>
      <p>The fridge defrost sensor (thermistor) has failed or disconnected. This is a failed-part code — a power cycle rarely clears it, so it needs a tech to replace the sensor.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 ICE MAKER CODES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Ice-maker codes</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Samsung's most common code cluster.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>39E / 39C — ice maker not completing its cycle</h3>
      <p>The ice maker fails to finish a harvest, usually because it's frozen or jammed. A forced defrost is the first thing to try.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>26E — ice-maker function error · 40E / 40C — ice-room fan</h3>
      <p>26E means the ice maker isn't making ice (check it's on, cold enough, and not frosted over); 40E is the ice-room fan, which is a tech replacement.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>8E, 14E — ice-maker sensors</h3>
      <p>Sensor faults in the ice-making system. These point to a specific failed part and are tech-level replacements.</p>
    </div>

    <p>Samsung ice makers frosting over is a well-documented pattern, so these codes often trace back to ice in the compartment rather than a dead part. The full walkthrough — forced defrost, the honest class-action facts, and the underlying fixes — is on our <a href="/services/ice-maker-repair/samsung-ice-maker-not-working/">Samsung ice maker not working</a> guide.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 COMMS + POWER + WATER -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · Communication, power, and water codes</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The rest of the common ones.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>88 88 — display / communication glitch</h3>
      <p>Usually caused by a power interruption during start-up. Unplug for a full minute and restore power; it often self-clears. Persistent = wiring or board.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>PC ER — communication error</h3>
      <p>The fridge components have lost communication, commonly from a loose wiring harness under the left door hinge on French-door models. A power cycle may help; the root cause usually needs a tech to reseat or replace the harness.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>84C — compressor · 85C — low voltage</h3>
      <p>84C is a compressor lock (the compressor won't start) — a sealed-system tech diagnosis. 85C is a low-voltage warning: check the fridge is on a dedicated outlet, not an extension cord or shared surge strip, before it's called a fault.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>76C — Autofill water detected</h3>
      <p>Water was detected in the Autofill pitcher compartment, usually a spill or overflow. Dry the compartment. If it recurs, the Autofill valve needs a tech.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="callout"><strong>A note on 41E / 42E:</strong> some sources label these as generic communication errors, but on Family Hub models Samsung describes them as a screen/software issue instead — the meaning is genuinely inconsistent across sources, so we won't pin a single definition on them. If you're seeing 41E or 42E, it's worth a diagnostic rather than a guess.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 SAFE FIX + TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · What to try, and when to call</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The safe steps first.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Rule out demo mode.</strong> If you see OF OF and the panel's lit but not cooling, hold the model's button combo ~8 seconds to exit.</li>
      <li><strong>Power cycle.</strong> Unplug for a full minute for 88 88 and transient glitches, then restore power.</li>
      <li><strong>Manual defrost for fan/ice codes.</strong> For 22E, 21E, 39E, or 26E, power off and let it defrost ~24 hours, then restart.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the outlet for 85C.</strong> Confirm a dedicated outlet, not an extension cord or shared strip.</li>
      <li><strong>Note whether the code returns.</strong> Cleared and gone = a glitch. Comes back = a failed part.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> exiting demo mode, power cycling, defrosting, drying the Autofill tray, and checking the outlet are all safe. Do not open the sealed system (84C / compressor), replace sensors or fans, or disassemble the wiring harness yourself. Sensor, compressor, and persistent codes are tech work.</p>

    <p>When a defrost and a power cycle don't hold — or the code is a sensor (5E, 8E, 14E), the compressor (84C), or a returning communication fault (PC ER) — the part needs testing and replacement. Our techs verify the simple causes first (demo mode, frost, a loose harness, the outlet) before quoting.</p>

    <p>More help: the <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/">refrigerator repair</a> hub, and if it simply isn't cooling, <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/not-cooling/">refrigerator not cooling</a>. LG owners: our <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/lg-error-codes/">LG refrigerator error codes</a> guide. Brand coverage on <a href="/brands/samsung-refrigerator-repair/">Samsung refrigerator repair</a> and the <a href="/brands/samsung/">Samsung appliance repair</a> pillar. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/refrigerator-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/refrigerator-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/irvine/refrigerator-repair/">Irvine</a> in most cases. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong> <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 FAQ -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Samsung refrigerator codes: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
